      "text": "To find the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\), we need to analyze the given angles in the diagram. We know the following:\n\n1. \\(\\angle ADB = 93^\\circ\\)\n2. \\(\\angle BDC = 130^\\circ\\)\n\nFirst, observe that \\(\\angle ADB\\) and \\(\\angle BDC\\) are angles at points \\(D\\) and \\(C\\) respectively. We need to find the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\), which is the remaining angle around point \\(C\\) in the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\).\n\nSince \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\) lie on a circle, the sum of the opposite angles in the cyclic quadrilateral is \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\angle ADB + \\angle BDC = 180^\\circ\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n93^\\circ + 130^\\circ = 223^\\circ\n\\]\nSince the sum of the angles around point \\(C\\) is \\(360^\\circ\\) (as \\(C\\) is a vertex of the cyclic quadrilateral), we can find \\(\\angle ACB\\) by subtracting the sum of the other three angles from \\(360^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = 360^\\circ - (\\angle ADB + \\angle BDC)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = 360^\\circ - 223^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = 137^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\) is \\(\\boxed{137}\\).",
