      "text": "1. We are given that triangle $ABC$ has one angle $B$ measuring $93^\\circ,$ another angle $A$ measuring $130^\\circ.$\n2. To find the measure of angle $\\angle ACB$, we can use the fact that the sum of the angles in a triangle is always $180^\\circ$.\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAC + \\angle ABC + \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n3. Substituting the given angles into the equation:\n   \\[\n   130^\\circ + 93^\\circ + \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n4. Solving for $\\angle ACB$:\n   \\[\n   223^\\circ + \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ - 223^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\angle ACB = -43^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nHowever, this result seems incorrect as angles cannot be negative. Let's recheck the problem detail.\n\nGiven:\n- $\\angle B = 93^\\circ$\n- $\\angle A = 130^\\circ$\n\nError in understanding or given details:\nThe correct approach is to use the fact that:\n- Triangle $ABC$ has one angle at $\\angle B = 93^\\circ$.\n- Angle adjacent to $\\angle A$ on the same side of line $AC$ is $\\angle B = 93^\\circ$.\n\nUsing the supplementary property for adjacent angles:\n\\[\n\\angle A + \\angle B = 180^\\circ\n\\]\nApproximate:\n\\[\n\\angle A + 93^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[\n\\angle A = 180^\\circ - 93^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{87^\\circ}\n\\]",
